#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171751 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/id #uuid "8a1be39d-6621-5d71-b1a4-3669d210ee8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6986" :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/42556eddb08beba40a46abcc4af06f69.png)\n\n1. $y' = x + y + 1$\n2. $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/c1cf6111a0a453b8105ef790e0748df2.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x \\to -1$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = x + y + 1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$, but\n$y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1} \\to -\\dfrac{y}{y+1} \\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad x \\to -1$,\nand hence $y'$ depends on $y$ along the line $x=-1$.\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$, and\n$y' = \\bigg \\mid \\dfrac{y}{x+1} \\bigg \\mid \\to \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad x \\to -1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171752 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f43aebf0-6aa9-58cf-9475-69f5af74d71f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6988" :question/problem "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/321b6eb66ca267d3844c0960f615026d.png)\n\n1. $y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n2. $y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n3. $y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/e8dd09383c592fafe22b0fd1c05a391a.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:$y > -1$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$y < -1$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x^2 + 2)(y + 1)$\nevaluates to zero at $y = -1$ only.\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x+2)(y+1)$\nevaluates to zero at $x = -2$ and $y = -1$, and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.$y > -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$y < -1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$${\\color{red}{-}}$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n$y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2 = (x+2)(y+1)^2$\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=-1$, but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.$y > -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$y < -1$${\\color{red}{-}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$$x< -2$ $x> -2$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is equation II."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171753 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/id #uuid "4dabeee5-1c46-5198-9c51-9099fb5df617" :question/math-academy-id "e-7126" :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/77e64d6b091c27df883b55b93e628c77.png)\n\n1. $y' = 2xy$\n2. $y' = -x(y-1)^2$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/b06ebab58bfb746f35ecba54930526a3.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=1$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:$y > 1$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$$\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ $y < 1$$\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$$x< 0$ $x> 0$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = 2xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0$.\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = -x(y-1)^2$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1$, but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.$y > 1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$${\\color{red}{-}}$ $y < 1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$ ${\\color{red}{-}}$$x< 0$ $x> 0$\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1$, and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.$y > 1$${\\color{blue}{+}}$${\\color{blue}{+}}$ $y < 1$${\\color{red}{-}}$ ${\\color{red}{-}}$$x< 0$ $x> 0$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171820 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/id #uuid "95746e3f-5cdf-58e2-8dbb-39b3cbe5cfc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9356" :question/problem "The differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by $y' = (x-a)(y-b)$. Find the value of $a + b$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/fd5112fd081ff4dbdf41ceb7d6b6ccc4.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/3327d1834e198b8eab50a19a54e5b9b1.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -2$ and $y = 2$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\ny' = (x+2)(y-2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = -2$, $b = 2$, and\n\n$$\na + b=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea62f2f-4926-5fd1-a6dc-043fffc13980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170918} {:answer/id #uuid "90b5cd5a-2b22-5c46-8f94-67c263f1329c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170919} {:answer/id #uuid "ef868f3c-79e8-589d-a876-a74c23a8c4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\sin (x + y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170920} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbcaab5-96a7-5e06-9994-bb3d87f0d2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{y}{1 + x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170921} {:answer/id #uuid "356f31ad-99dc-5f76-a79f-fcc60474b0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = y - x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b5cd5a-2b22-5c46-8f94-67c263f1329c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eda099f-580a-595e-991a-58faa4af3438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170917}] :question/id #uuid "1eda5ee0-9c5c-5074-a654-a6372da78ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49940" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-49940.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1f7b36e-81b6-5f26-9623-ea06a47ecbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170930} {:answer/id #uuid "eb42cf7c-c7db-5cd0-9626-746a1549b06c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170931} {:ans